1
00:00:00,817 --> 00:00:03,670
One other interesting thing you can do with

2
00:00:03,863 --> 00:00:07,213
Ollama is save chat sessions.

3
00:00:08,046 --> 00:00:11,701
And actually chat session is not entirely

4
00:00:11,733 --> 00:00:12,791
the correct term,

5
00:00:13,416 --> 00:00:16,318
even though Ollama itself calls it saving a

6
00:00:16,318 --> 00:00:18,834
session. But what you'll actually do, as you

7
00:00:18,850 --> 00:00:20,117
will see over the next minutes,

8
00:00:20,710 --> 00:00:24,525
is you can save a copy of a model that has

9
00:00:24,557 --> 00:00:27,973
certain settings and parameters baked in.

10
00:00:28,487 --> 00:00:29,885
For example, here I could say, hi,

11
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:31,460
I'm Max.

12
00:00:32,199 --> 00:00:33,919
So I'm sharing my name with

13
00:00:34,578 --> 00:00:36,586
Ollama, or I'm putting it into the chat

14
00:00:36,586 --> 00:00:38,048
history to be more precise.

15
00:00:39,157 --> 00:00:41,005
Now, as you see, if you type slash question

16
00:00:41,005 --> 00:00:43,640
mark, you can now save your current session.

17
00:00:44,717 --> 00:00:46,211
So here I'll say save

18
00:00:47,047 --> 00:00:48,541
S1 for session 1.

19
00:00:49,682 --> 00:00:53,076
And it says created new model S1 here. Now

20
00:00:53,076 --> 00:00:53,988
I'll quit here.

21
00:00:55,111 --> 00:00:55,974
And if I now run

22
00:00:56,901 --> 00:00:57,748
this model again,

23
00:00:58,753 --> 00:01:00,402
I can say load S1

24
00:01:02,214 --> 00:01:04,397
and I loaded that other session

25
00:01:04,963 --> 00:01:07,162
or model. And I'll get back to why it's

26
00:01:07,162 --> 00:01:08,293
called a model in a second.

27
00:01:09,241 --> 00:01:11,120
But if I now ask what's my name, it

28
00:01:11,265 --> 00:01:13,434
remembers that my name is Max, even though I

29
00:01:13,434 --> 00:01:15,764
did not share that in this chat session

30
00:01:15,764 --> 00:01:18,231
here, but I did share it in that session I

31
00:01:18,231 --> 00:01:19,807
saved. The S1 session.

32
00:01:21,670 --> 00:01:23,409
Now, what's important to understand though

33
00:01:23,409 --> 00:01:27,066
is that technically Ollama doesn't just save

34
00:01:27,098 --> 00:01:28,806
and load the chat session.

35
00:01:29,337 --> 00:01:31,015
Instead, it created a copy

36
00:01:31,759 --> 00:01:33,021
of this model here

37
00:01:33,798 --> 00:01:37,115
where it basically saved the previous chat

38
00:01:37,115 --> 00:01:37,552
session

39
00:01:38,231 --> 00:01:39,251
in the model

40
00:01:39,945 --> 00:01:43,577
data or in the model initialization data so

41
00:01:43,577 --> 00:01:46,721
that whenever you load or run that saved

42
00:01:46,817 --> 00:01:49,191
model, it would be initialized with the

43
00:01:49,239 --> 00:01:51,293
entire chat history it had

44
00:01:51,870 --> 00:01:55,143
when I saved that session, when I saved that

45
00:01:55,255 --> 00:01:58,287
model. So it's a copy of that Gemma model

46
00:01:58,865 --> 00:02:01,560
with the exact same configuration, but also

47
00:02:01,656 --> 00:02:04,544
with the chat history I had at the point of

48
00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:06,020
time where I saved it.

49
00:02:06,629 --> 00:02:08,217
That's important to understand.

50
00:02:09,116 --> 00:02:10,961
And therefore, of course, you can use that

51
00:02:11,314 --> 00:02:14,875
save command not just to save a chat history

52
00:02:15,132 --> 00:02:18,067
for future use, but also to save anything

53
00:02:18,180 --> 00:02:20,329
else you changed about this chat session.

54
00:02:21,227 --> 00:02:23,200
For example, if I set a system prompt here

55
00:02:23,729 --> 00:02:27,691
and I say, you are a friendly customer

56
00:02:28,157 --> 00:02:29,504
service agent,

57
00:02:30,707 --> 00:02:31,958
don't grant any

58
00:02:32,792 --> 00:02:33,321
refunds.

59
00:02:34,476 --> 00:02:36,738
If I set this system message

60
00:02:38,069 --> 00:02:38,615
and I then

61
00:02:39,224 --> 00:02:39,978
save this

62
00:02:41,438 --> 00:02:44,213
and I overwrote my old model S1,

63
00:02:45,240 --> 00:02:46,256
If I now quit this,

64
00:02:48,026 --> 00:02:48,801
and again, I run

65
00:02:50,286 --> 00:02:52,288
my model here and I type load S1,

66
00:02:53,047 --> 00:02:54,306
and there would be a different way of

67
00:02:54,322 --> 00:02:56,082
loading that saved model, but for now I'll

68
00:02:56,115 --> 00:02:56,760
do it like this.

69
00:02:57,562 --> 00:02:59,381
If I do that and I then type show system,

70
00:02:59,655 --> 00:03:02,215
you'll see that that system message was also

71
00:03:02,215 --> 00:03:04,438
saved. So that's one way of setting

72
00:03:05,033 --> 00:03:07,756
a system message and any other kind of

73
00:03:07,853 --> 00:03:10,030
parameter settings you might wanna set and

74
00:03:10,030 --> 00:03:12,594
maybe even create a chat history that should

75
00:03:12,626 --> 00:03:13,094
be saved

76
00:03:13,659 --> 00:03:14,288
and then save it in a separate model that

77
00:03:14,288 --> 00:03:14,465
you can reuse.

78
00:03:17,543 --> 00:03:19,408
So that if you know that you have certain

79
00:03:19,456 --> 00:03:21,498
settings you wanna use over and over again,

80
00:03:21,707 --> 00:03:23,669
you can conveniently save them, load them,

81
00:03:23,958 --> 00:03:26,257
and you don't have to reset them every time

82
00:03:26,675 --> 00:03:29,232
you start a new chat history. So that is a

83
00:03:29,328 --> 00:03:30,277
useful feature,

84
00:03:31,016 --> 00:03:32,817
but it's also a feature that's worth a

85
00:03:32,882 --> 00:03:33,525
closer look.
